INT. ROOTHOUSE TEAROOM - AFTERNOON

A parlor grown inside the hollow of an immense oak. The walls are living bark, polished by decades of elbows. Honey-colored light falls through a knothole window, and tiny lanterns of captured fireflies hang from the ceiling on spider-silk. A kettle on a mushroom-stool burner sings.

ALDOUS, 70, a wizard in a tea-stained cardigan, pours from a pot shaped like a sleeping badger. SEREN, 20s, an apprentice in a patched green cloak, stirs her cup and stirs it again. PIP, 9, a boy with soot on his chin, sits under the table feeding crumbs to something invisible.

ALDOUS
Milk?

SEREN
Please.

PIP
Is it a lot of milk?

ALDOUS
It's enough.

SEREN
It's a bit much.

ALDOUS
You said please.

PIP
Seren said please, I said please, Master Aldous said please.

SEREN
Nobody said please to the cat.

ALDOUS
We have a cat?

PIP
We have a cat under the table.

SEREN
We don't have a cat.

PIP
It's a very quiet cat.

Aldous passes Seren her tea. She holds it, not drinking. She watches the knothole.

A shadow crosses the window. The door, a round slab of bark on a leather hinge, swings inward. WIDOW HARROWGATE, 80s, small and square in a black bonnet, leans on a cane carved from a single antler. She has the look of a woman who has been on her way for a very long time.

ALDOUS
Mrs. Harrowgate.

WIDOW HARROWGATE
Aldous. You've aged.

ALDOUS
You're early.

WIDOW HARROWGATE
I'm precisely on time. You were always late.

SEREN
Tea?

WIDOW HARROWGATE
Not today, girl.

PIP
Is she the one with the owl?

SEREN
Pip.

WIDOW HARROWGATE
I am the one with the owl.

ALDOUS
Sit, Hester.

WIDOW HARROWGATE
I'll stand.

She doesn't. She lowers herself into the nearest chair, set at the head of the table like a throne. The firefly lanterns brighten very slightly. Nobody says anything. Aldous refills his cup. Seren stirs. Pip, under the table, tugs at the invisible cat's tail. The kettle sings on.
